{"id":"cell_diamond","name":"Diamond unit cell","formula":"C","group":"Solid state: unit cells","labels":{"electron":"Lattice type","shape":"Coordination number","hyb":"Atoms per unit cell (Z)","angle":"Packing efficiency","polar":"Examples"},"electron":"Diamond cubic: fcc with half of the tetrahedral holes also occupied by C","shape":"4  (tetrahedral)","hyb":"8  (8 × ⅛ + 6 × ½ + 4)","angle":"34%  (an open structure)","polar":"Diamond, Si, Ge, grey tin","vdw":{"C":0.875},"note":"Each carbon is sp³ hybridised and bonded to four others (C–C 1.54 Å) in a three-dimensional covalent network, so diamond is the hardest natural substance, has a very high melting point and does not conduct electricity (no free electrons) yet conducts heat well. Graphite (sp², layers 3.35 Å apart) conducts. Solid sticks show the C–C bonds. Dashed lines mark the unit-cell edges, not bonds.","atoms":[["C",-1.784,-1.784,-1.784],["C",-1.784,-1.784,1.784],["C",-1.784,1.784,-1.784],["C",-1.784,1.784,1.784],["C",1.784,-1.784,-1.784],["C",1.784,-1.784,1.784],["C",1.784,1.784,-1.784],["C",1.784,1.784,1.784],["C",0.0,0.0,-1.784],["C",0.0,0.0,1.784],["C",0.0,-1.784,0.0],["C",0.0,1.784,0.0],["C",-1.784,0.0,0.0],["C",1.784,0.0,0.0],["C",-0.892,-0.892,-0.892],["C",0.892,0.892,-0.892],["C",0.892,-0.892,0.892],["C",-0.892,0.892,0.892]],"bonds":[[0,1,0.5],[0,2,0.5],[0,4,0.5],[1,3,0.5],[1,5,0.5],[2,3,0.5],[2,6,0.5],[3,7,0.5],[4,5,0.5],[4,6,0.5],[5,7,0.5],[6,7,0.5],[0,14,1],[3,17,1],[5,16,1],[6,15,1],[8,14,1],[8,15,1],[9,16,1],[9,17,1],[10,14,1],[10,16,1],[11,15,1],[11,17,1],[12,14,1],[12,17,1],[13,15,1],[13,16,1]],"lonePairs":[]}
